> below that would indicate a problem with that application! We have
parallelized
> processes which show 385% CPU (on a 4 CPU ES40 with top). A value below
> 99% just indicates that the process is waiting for some resource, so the
> CPU is not used a portion of the time. This is more usual for system level
> services. If you see such processes with 99% for a long time without
> interruption, then you may deduce an error condition.
> It just depends on what the program was designed to do.

Precisely. The original post was about BigBrother scripts. Since I'm not
familiar with BB, I cannot vouch that it is *not* supposed to be 100%
CPU-bound. However, I'd expect it to be talking to I/O from time to time,
thus reducing CPU load.

Nix.




